    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. some of these bugs feel really random though, almost like they're hidden deep in the code or just popping up out of nowhere. is there any way to predict these kinds of \"phantom\" errors before they actually hit the user, or are they just something you have to hunt down manually during the audit?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While you cannot predict every obscure bug perfectly without testing, you can identify risks by analyzing recent code changes, specifically looking for keyboard event handlers or complex font rendering issues that often cause accessibilty failures. However, because these \"phantom\" errors frequently depend on specific browser-OS interactions or timely mouse movements, manual testing with multiple invisible and specialized user simulations remains the most reliable method to catch them before launch. Consider partnering with specialized software vendors or non-native testers to uncover these deep-seated edge cases that your in-house automated suite might miss.",
      "source": "target",
